# Tools

Every tool has **three parts**: definition, async handler, registration in `src/tools/index.ts`.

## Modules

Place tools under domain folders:

- `costumers/` — customer lookups and account actions
- `sales/` — memberships, discounts, leads, allied commerces, categories
- `documentation/` — support logs / operational traceability

## Definition + handler

```typescript
export const getExampleTool: Tool = {
  name: 'get_example',
  description: 'Detailed description for the AI agent: when to use, what it returns',
  inputSchema: {
    type: 'object',
    properties: {
      id: { type: 'number', description: 'Entity id' },
    },
    required: ['id'],
  },
}

export async function handleGetExample(args: { id: number }) {
  const service = new ExampleService()
  const result = await service.getById(args.id)

  if (!result.success || !result.data) {
    return {
      content: [{ type: 'text' as const, text: result.error?.message || 'Error' }],
      isError: true,
    }
  }

  return {
    content: [{ type: 'text' as const, text: JSON.stringify(result.data, null, 2) }],
  }
}
```

## Registration (`src/tools/index.ts`)

1. Import tool + handler
2. Add tool to `tools` array
3. Add `case` in `handleToolCall` switch

## Rules

- `name` in snake_case, unique and semantic
- Descriptions must be rich — they drive agent tool selection
- Always define full `inputSchema`; re-validate critical args in the handler
- Always check `result.success` before returning data
- Prefer researching the CRM endpoint first (`crm-endpoint-research` / `crm-api-researcher`) then implement via `mcp-tool-creator`
